{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB11576 - MONDO_1639",
  "verbalization": "Ferric Oxide is implicated in the pathogenesis of Deficiency Anemia through a series of interconnected indications. The evidence suggests that Ferric Oxide is associated with Microcytic Anemia and Iron Deficiency Anemia, both of which are linked to Ferumoxytol, a drug that further links to Deficiency Anemia. This creates a pathway where Ferric Oxide may act as a precursor or mediator in the development of Iron Deficiency Anemia, potentially through the regulation of iron metabolism or the stimulation of iron absorption. The presence of multiple indirect pathways, including the use of Ferrous Gluconate and Ferumoxytol, highlights a possible role for Ferric Oxide in the broader context of iron-related disorders. The integration of these pathways underscores the importance of Ferric Oxide in the context of iron deficiency, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
